For the Docker recipes, pin the release by replacing the rolling tag with
`<release>-<backend>`. For example, use `zmlai/llmd:20261112.0-cuda` for the
Qwen3.8-27B CUDA recipe. Find versioned tags and digests on
[Docker Hub](https://hub.docker.com/r/zmlai/llmd/tags). For Metal, `brew install`
uses the version currently in the [ZML tap](https://github.com/zml/homebrew-zml).
The [20261106.0 release notes](https://zml.ai/posts/llmd-20261106.0/) list
additional model families. This catalog covers specific combinations with
published launch instructions.

# [Keep the checkpoint files together]($heading.id('keep-the-checkpoint-files-together'))

Point `--model` at the directory containing `config.json`. LLMD reads
`model_type` from this file to select a model implementation. Keep the files
from the same revision together.

- Include `tokenizer.json` and the published tokenizer configuration.
- Include the Safetensors weights. Keep any supplied Safetensors index and
  every shard it references in the same relative locations.
- Keep ancillary files from the checkpoint, such as `chat_template.jinja`
  or `generation_config.json`. Chat templates can also be supplied through
  `tokenizer_config.json`.

See the [LFM3-1.7B FP8 files](https://huggingface.co/LiquidAI/LFM3-1.7B-FP8/tree/main)
for an example of a complete checkpoint.

# [Load from Hugging Face]($heading.id('load-from-hugging-face'))

Use `--model=hf://LiquidAI/LFM3-1.7B-FP8` for the public checkpoint in the
quickstart. LLMD reads the files from Hugging Face at startup. The repository
defaults to `main`.
Use `--model=hf://org/repo@revision` with an exact commit when reproducing a
deployment.

For a [gated repository](https://huggingface.co/docs/hub/models-gated), request
access on the model page and export `HF_TOKEN` for an account with read
access. Add `-e HF_TOKEN` before the Docker image name to forward it. Pass the
repository URI as `--model` after the image name. See
[Environment variables]($link.page('reference/environment')) for token lookup
order.

# [Download a checkpoint]($heading.id('download-a-checkpoint'))

Download weights to a writable host directory to reuse the same checkpoint
across container launches. Install [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/getting-started/installation/),
then download the complete repository for the CUDA quickstart.

```sh
uvx --from huggingface_hub hf download LiquidAI/LFM3-1.7B-FP8 \
  --local-dir "${HOME}/models/LiquidAI/LFM3-1.7B-FP8"
```

Use the same directory layout for a Gemma checkpoint.

```sh
uvx --from huggingface_hub hf download google/gemma-4-31B-it \
  --local-dir "${HOME}/models/google/gemma-4-31B-it"
```

For a gated or private repository, confirm your account has read access and
run `uvx --from huggingface_hub hf auth login` or export `HF_TOKEN` before
downloading. Add `--revision` with the commit hash to pin the files, and keep
each revision in its own directory. The
[Hugging Face CLI guide](https://huggingface.co/docs/huggingface_hub/en/guides/cli#download-to-a-local-folder)
covers download options.

# [Load from a local directory]($heading.id('load-from-a-local-directory'))

On a CUDA host configured as in the quickstart, mount the downloaded LFM3
directory read-only at `/model` inside the container. To serve another compatible
checkpoint, set the mount source to the absolute path to its directory.

```sh
docker run --rm --name llmd \
  -p 127.0.0.1:8000:8000 --gpus=all --shm-size=256GB \
  --mount "type=bind,source=${HOME}/models/LiquidAI/LFM3-1.7B-FP8,target=/model,readonly" \
  zmlai/llmd:20261106.0-cuda --model=/model --model-name=LFM3-1.7B-FP8
```

For a native installation, pass the local path directly with
`llmd --model="${HOME}/models/LiquidAI/LFM3-1.7B-FP8"`. Use the
[Docker launch workspace]($link.page('llmd/launch')) to generate a command
for either storage workflow.

# [Load from S3 or Google Cloud Storage]($heading.id('load-from-s3-or-google-cloud-storage'))

Keep the checkpoint directory structure under the bucket prefix. Replace the
`--model` value in your platform's startup command with the corresponding URI.

| Storage | Model argument | Docker credentials |
| --- | --- | --- |
| S3 | `--model=s3://my-bucket/llm/checkpoint` | For a private bucket, export `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID` and `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY` and pass both with `-e` before the image. Forward endpoint or region overrides if your store needs them. |
| Google Cloud Storage | `--model=gs://my-bucket/llm/checkpoint` | For file-based credentials, mount a supported credentials file read-only and set `GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS` to its path inside the container. |

See [Environment variables]($link.page('reference/environment')) for credential
precedence, supported credential types, and an example credentials mount.
The [LLMD product page](https://zml.ai/llmd/) describes the supported storage
URI schemes.

# [Verify the loaded model]($heading.id('verify-the-loaded-model'))

Check `GET /v1/models` after startup and use the returned ID in a chat request.
Use `--model-name=<name>` to keep that ID stable when changing storage paths.

The catalog recipes were checked against their published sources. Local
hardware validation is pending. Record the LLMD version or image digest,
checkpoint revision, accelerator and driver, and a successful request when
validating a deployment.

